    q: 'Which port should I use when shipping from China to Germany?',
    a: 'Use Hamburg (UN/LOCODE DEHAM) as the German seaport destination — it is Germany\u2019s largest port and a China-rail terminus, with a published throughput of ≈ 7.7m TEU (2023) and key terminals HHLA CTA, Eurogate and Altenwerder. Rotterdam (NLRTM, ≈ 13.4m TEU 2023) is the deeper-water alternative when you are consolidating EU-wide distribution or shipping on the largest vessels, but Hamburg is the correct first choice for German-market cargo.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I ship China-to-Germany by rail?',
    a: 'Yes — rail from Xi\u2019an or Chengdu to Hamburg runs roughly 18–22 days, versus the roughly 30–38-day sea window, so it cuts about 8–16 days at roughly two to three times the sea rate. Germany is a primary New Silk Road terminus, with trains from Xi\u2019an, Chengdu and Chongqing arriving at Hamburg and other German hubs, so price the financing cost of the extra sea days against the rail premium before defaulting to ocean.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need an EORI to import into Germany?',
    a: 'Yes — one EORI (Economic Operators Registration and Identification) number is required for any import into the EU, and it must exist before your goods arrive; zero German imports clear without it. It is the single most common first-import stumbling block for China-to-Germany cargo, so start the registration before the vessel or train departs, not while it is discharging.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the VAT rate in Germany?',
    a: 'Germany applies a 19% standard VAT rate, with a 7% reduced rate on some essentials. Import VAT is charged on the duty-inclusive value, so the effective tax is slightly above the headline 19% — budget the compound (CIF + duty) × 1.19 rather than a flat 19% on CIF.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between FCL and LCL for China to Germany?',
    a: 'FCL is priced per container at 20ft $1,200–$4,600 and 40ft $1,600–$6,500 to Hamburg (MEDIUM), while LCL is priced per cubic metre at $60–$150 (MEDIUM); the planning break-even is around 15 CBM, below which LCL is usually cheaper and above which FCL starts to win. That is a LOW-confidence rule of thumb, so request quotes on both sides of the split before deciding.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the €150 de-minimis threshold and how does IOSS work?',
    a: 'The EU abolished the €22 duty-free threshold in 2021, so import VAT now applies from the first euro on most consignments. The €150 figure is the VAT-related threshold, and the IOSS (Import One-Stop Shop) scheme simplifies VAT collection on sub-€150 consignments sold to EU buyers. Confirm the current thresholds and whether IOSS applies to your channel with German customs before relying on these figures.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What documents are needed for German customs clearance?',
    a: 'The core German clearance set is four documents plus one classification — a commercial invoice, bill of lading (or rail consignment note for rail cargo), packing list and the correct 10-digit TARIC code, backed by your EORI number. Regulated products also need CE marking evidence, and chemical substances need REACH registration — confirm the exact document set for your product class with a licensed German broker.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need SABER or SASO to import into Germany?',
    a: 'No — SABER and SASO apply to exactly zero German or EU imports: they are Saudi-only conformity systems. Do not copy a Saudi SABER/SASO step into a German quote. Germany uses CE marking for regulated products and REACH for chemical substances instead, and any product-specific approval should be confirmed with German customs or your broker.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are CE marking and REACH, and do they apply?',
    a: 'CE marking is the EU conformity mark required for a wide range of regulated products — electronics, toys, machinery and medical devices, among others — declaring the product meets EU requirements. REACH is the EU chemical regulation requiring registration for chemical substances. Whether either applies depends on your product class, so confirm the applicability and the required technical file before shipment.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do demurrage and detention differ in Germany?',
    a: 'Demurrage and detention are two separate clocks with two separate payees: demurrage is the charge for port free-time overstay after discharge, and detention is the charge for container free-time overstay after collection. German free-time periods and per-day rates are not published in the verified snapshot — confirm both allowances in writing before booking and file the declaration promptly so neither clock runs.',
