          { label: 'Europe', href: '/europe/' },
          { label: 'Shipping from China to Germany' },
        ]}
      />
      <span class="badge badge--amber">September 2026 updated</span>
      <h1>Shipping from China to Germany: Hamburg routing, 30–38-day sea &amp; 18–22-day rail transit, EU duty &amp; 19% VAT</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A one-page freight brief for importers moving cargo from Shanghai, Ningbo-Zhoushan, Shenzhen
        and the wider China origin set to Hamburg and the German market — with the FCL-vs-LCL decision,
        the roughly 30–38-day sea window via the Cape, the 18–22-day rail alternative, EU common duty
        plus 19% VAT, the EORI gate, and the hidden destination charges that shape the German landed cost.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="tldr">
        <p><strong>TL;DR:</strong> Rail to Hamburg at 18–22 days compresses the 30–38-day sea window by roughly 8–16 days for inland/Ruhr cargo, at roughly 2–3× the sea rate. Germany's 19% VAT lands on (CIF + duty), so a €10,000 CIF with 5% duty is €1,995 VAT — €95 more than a flat 19% on CIF. Hamburg's ≈7.7m TEU (2023) is roughly 57% of Rotterdam's ≈13.4m TEU, making Hamburg the German-market call and Rotterdam the EU-wide consolidation call.</p>
      </blockquote>
      <p class="small muted">
        Confidence badges separate verified figures from indicative planning notes. Germany\u2019s FCL/LCL
        ranges come from the WorldFreightHub Europe route data (MEDIUM), while rail, air, express and DDP
