  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. Why Germany deserves its own China routing lens</h2>
    <p>
      Germany is the volume play in Europe, and the difference from the Gulf corridor is structural.
      The country data shows <strong>Berlin</strong> as the capital, the currency as <strong>EUR</strong>,
      and a <strong>19% standard VAT</strong> layered on top of the <strong>EU Common Customs
      Tariff</strong> — a duty line that is uniform across every member state and varies only by HS code,
      typically <strong>0–12% for consumer goods</strong> with no China–EU free-trade agreement in force.
    </p>
    <p>
      The gate is not the duty rate; it is the <strong>EORI number</strong>. The Economic Operators
      Registration and Identification number is mandatory for any import into the EU, and it must exist
      <em>before</em> the goods arrive — not after. That single administrative step stops more first-time
      importers than any tariff line, and it is why this page treats EORI as a compliance prerequisite
      rather than an afterthought.
    </p>
    <p>
      Germany is also where <strong>deep-sea and China rail physically meet</strong>. Hamburg takes New
      Silk Road trains from Xi\u2019an, Chengdu and Chongqing alongside ocean vessels, which makes the
      sea-versus-rail decision a genuine planning option rather than a theoretical one. On the commercial
      side, the honest position is that <strong>FCL and LCL ranges are published</strong> for the Hamburg
      corridor (MEDIUM confidence), while rail, air, express and DDP dollar figures are not — so those
      lines stay explicit request-for-quote markers instead of borrowed numbers.
    </p>
  </section>

