  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>6. Cost composition and the hidden charges that competitors miss</h2>
      <p>
        Germany\u2019s statutory stack is only the visible top layer: <strong>EU common duty on
        CIF</strong> (typically 0–12% by HS code), then <strong>19% VAT on the duty-inclusive
        base</strong>. The commercial risk sits below that line in operational charges that the verified
        snapshot does not quantify — THC, documentation, inspection, port storage, demurrage and
        detention. This is where a cheap freight quote can become an expensive clearance.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        <strong>Baseline statutory landed cost = (CIF + duty) × 1.19.</strong><br />
        Duty varies by HS code, so there is no single multiplier like the Gulf\u2019s 5%. For an
        illustrative USD 10,000 CIF shipment at an assumed 5% duty: USD 500 duty gives a USD 10,500
        duty-inclusive base, then 19% VAT of USD 1,995, giving USD 12,495 before destination fees. Rates
        and duty lines are subject to change — verify the current duty line and VAT treatment with German
        Customs before relying on this example.
      </div>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Full cost stack</h3>
      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'item', label: 'Cost component' },
          { key: 'who', label: 'Who charges it' },
          { key: 'magnitude', label: 'Indicative magnitude' },
