  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>At a 5% duty assumption, a €10,000 CIF shipment clears at €12,495: €500 duty (5% × 10,000), €1,995 VAT on the €10,500 duty-inclusive base (19% × 10,500), a 24.95% uplift (1.05 × 1.19 = 1.2495).</li>
      <li>Germany's 19% VAT on CIF + duty equals 19.95% of CIF (19% × 1.05), so it adds €95 more than flat 19% on €10,000 CIF (1,995 − 1,900).</li>
      <li>Rail's 18–22-day window saves 8–16 days versus 30–38-day sea, but that saving is only 21–53% of the sea window (8 ÷ 38 = 21%; 16 ÷ 30 = 53%) for 2–3× the sea rate — pay the premium only when stockout or perishability costs justify it.</li>
      <li>The 15 CBM heuristic is fragile: at LCL US$60–150/CBM, 15 CBM costs US$900–2,250 (15 × 60 = 900; 15 × 150 = 2,250), already exceeding the US$1,200 20ft FCL floor by US$1,050 at the top of the LCL range.</li>
      <li>Hamburg's 7.7m TEU is 7.7 ÷ 13.4 = 57.5% of Rotterdam's 13.4m TEU, so treating Hamburg as an EU-wide consolidation gateway forfeits 42.5% of the Rotterdam scale; use Hamburg for German inland/Ruhr cargo and Rotterdam for multi-country consolidation.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

